#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
harness_registry — the fleet-wide AI HARNESS + SCHEDULE inventory.

`harness.py` (singular) watches the hub's own six layers; THIS module watches
what is DEPLOYED: every AI harness call site and every cron schedule across
every registry repo, graded against the `harnesses:` contract in
_data/fleet.yml. It is the central-management surface the fleet lacked — the
one place that answers, for ~40 repos at once:

  OBSERVE    which repos carry which harness workflows (mention handlers,
             scheduled agents), on which claude-code-action ref, with which
             auth shape (OAuth-first is the house convention), which model and
             turn budget, and which agent-context kit version stamped them;
  SCHEDULES  every cron in the fleet — AI and not — normalized into estimated
             fires/day and a single UTC calendar, so standing load is a table
             rather than 40 repos' worth of workflow files;
  THROUGHPUT estimated scheduled AI runs/day (per repo and fleet-wide) versus
             the caps the contract declares, plus UTC-hour adjacency — the
             three Claude loops already share one OAuth account's rate limit;
  COST       spend trends from the committed usage ledgers (ai_usage.yml,
             actions_usage.yml): week-over-week growth and a projected monthly
             figure versus the declared budget ceilings;
  DRIFT      per-repo baseline coverage (mention handler, agent context,
             OAuth secret) and kit-version drift — the `gaps` feed that
             fanout.yml (kit: harness) and `dash harnesses deploy --gaps` target.
  FACTORY    GitFactory (bamr87/gitorio) provenance, in that app's own
             vocabulary: which repos carry a `.factory/` design (blueprints),
             which workflows are compiled LINES (the `⚙ GENERATED BY
             GITFACTORY` header + blueprint path/hash), and which scheduled
             agents are gated on a `vars.*_ENABLED` kill switch — the
             `gate` pattern that lets a line be disarmed without a commit.
  STANDARD   every harness graded against the harness standard
             (`harnesses.standard` in _data/fleet.yml): the ROUTE it reaches a
             model by — a claude-code-action step, the hub's claude-run action
             or ai-lane workflow, a bare `claude -p`, a vendored runner, or
             another provider's key — and the controls the constitution asks
             of it (pinned model, dollar and turn caps, kill switch, ordered
             auth, trusted-author gate, least privilege, timeout, off-the-hour
             cron). Each harness carries what it is missing and the LEVER that
             rebuilds it: a kit upgrade, a kit re-seed, a move onto the
             reusable lane, a blueprint recompile, a hub fix, or retirement.

This file is a CONSUMED INTERFACE: GitFactory's Fleet Studio (gitorio
specs/012-fleet-studio) reads the committed output as its fleet roster and
contract source. The `schema:` marker is the compatibility promise —
additive changes are free; a rename or removal bumps the marker
(docs/HARNESS-OPS.md, "Consumed by GitFactory").

Two halves, split on network:

  scan       PyGithub over each owned registry repo's .github/workflows/ (same
             auth conventions as fleet_triage/ai_usage_collector). Degrades:
             without a token (or with --offline) the previous committed scan
             is REUSED and only the offline analytics refresh, so a local
             `dash harnesses` run never needs credentials.
  analytics  pure functions over committed inputs — deterministic, offline,
             fixture-tested (test_harness_registry.py). Cron math is an
             ESTIMATE (documented per function), good for load planning, not
             for predicting exact fire times.

Output: _data/harness_registry.yml (committed, refreshed daily by
fleet-pulse.yml's pulse job, rendered at /harnesses/). Everything here is
read-only observation: a finding is an attention item for a human or for the
fan-out's dry-run PR path — never an auto-write into a fleet repo.
Full doc: docs/HARNESS-OPS.md
"""
